{"id":"3014af5d-e250-49ba-878a-6ffabf2fa158","object":"chat.completion","created":1785034304,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    if \\\"capacity\\\" not in case or \\\"trials\\\" not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing required keys: capacity, trials\\\")\\n    capacity = case[\\\"capacity\\\"]\\n    trials = case[\\\"trials\\\"]\\n    if not isinstance(capacity, int) or capacity < 0:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Capacity must be non-negative integer\\\")\\n    if not isinstance(trials, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Trials must be a list\\\")\\n\\n    memory = []\\n\\n    for idx, trial in enumerate(trials):\\n        if not isinstance(trial, dict) or not {\\\"signature\\\", \\\"reflection\\\", \\\"success\\\"}.issubset(trial.keys()):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Each trial must have signature, reflection, success\\\")\\n        entry = {\\n            \\\"last_trial\\\": idx,\\n            \\\"reflection\\\": trial[\\\"reflection\\\"],\\n            \\\"signature\\\": trial[\\\"signature\\\"],\\n            \\\"success\\\": trial[\\\"success\\\"]\\n        }\\n        if not trial[\\\"success\\\"]:\\n            replaced = False\\n            for i, mem in enumerate(memory):\\n                if not mem[\\\"success\\\"] and mem[\\\"signature\\\"] == trial[\\\"signature\\\"]:\\n                    memory[i] = entry\\n                    replaced = True\\n                    break\\n            if not replaced:\\n                memory.append(entry)\\n        else:\\n            memory.append(entry)\\n\\n    if len(memory) > capacity:\\n        failures = [m for m in memory if not m[\\\"success\\\"]]\\n        successes = [m for m in memory if m[\\\"success\\\"]]\\n        failures.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[\\\"last_trial\\\"], x[\\\"signature\\\"]))\\n        successes.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[\\\"last_trial\\\"], x[\\\"signature\\\"]))\\n        sorted_all = failures + successes\\n        kept = sorted_all[:capacity]\\n        kept.sort(key=lambda x: x[\\\"last_trial\\\"])\\n        memory = kept\\n\\n    signatures = [m[\\\"signature\\\"] for m in memory]\\n    return {\\\"memory\\\": memory, \\\"signatures\\\": signatures}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for task AGENT-RF-01. The case payload has capacity (integer) and trials (list of objects with signature, reflection, success). We must maintain an episodic reflection memory with deduplication by failure signature (only failures? or all? The description says \"deduplicate memory by failure signature, retaining the newest reflection.\" But also \"Prioritize failures before successes, then recency and signature.\" This implies memory stores reflections (maybe both successes and failures). However, the dedup by failure signature suggests we only deduplicate failures? But the memory may contain both.\n\nFrom the output schema, memory is an array of objects with last_trial (integer), reflection, signature, success. So we store every trial? But capacity binding: we need to keep at most capacity entries. Process in chronological order. For each trial, we decide whether to add to memory. The description: \"Update and truncate an episodic reflection memory from registered trial outcomes, feedback, and reflection records\". The goal: \"When capacity binds, prioritize failures before successes, then recency and signature.\" So we need to keep up to capacity entries, preferring failures over successes. Among same type, recency (later trial index?) and signature (presumably alphabetical or something? \"signature\" is a string; we need to define ordering. Typically signature could be any string. We need a deterministic ordering. Since we cannot rely on hash iteration, we need to define a sort key. The description: \"All ranking and traversal ties use the source position or identifier specified above; never depend on hash-map iteration.\" For ties, we need to use the trial index (source position) as tiebreaker? Or \"recency\" likely means trial index (chronological order). So when capacity is exceeded, we remove entries in order: remove successes before failures, then among same type remove oldest (smallest trial index) first? Or remove oldest? The phrase \"prioritize failures before successes, then recency and signature\" suggests that when we need to prune, we keep failures, and if we still have too many failures, we keep the most recent ones (high trial index) and also consider signature? Possibly signature as secondary sort after recency? But it's ambiguous.\n\nAlso deduplication: \"Deduplicate memory by failure signature, retaining the newest reflection.\" This likely applies to failures only: if a failure trial has a signature that already exists in memory (as a failure), we replace older entry with newer (keeping trial index of newer). So dedup is before capacity bound? Or after? Usually you dedup then truncate.\n\nProcessing steps: iterate trials in order. For each trial, decide whether to add to memory. Possibly only failures? The description says \"memory from registered trial outcomes, feedback, and reflection records\". But then \"deduplicate memory by failure signature\" suggests memory only stores failures? But output schema includes success boolean, so it stores both. However, many examples of Reflexion memory store only failures (bad experiences). But the output schema includes success, so we must store all trials? The requirement: \"Return complete retained entries and their signatures.\" So memory should contain entries for which we retain.\n\nGiven the complexity, we need to infer from the public fixtures. Let's examine the provided cases and try to deduce expected output. But we don't have expected outputs. However we can reason: The task is \"AGENT-RF-01\" which is \"Reflexion Failures\" maybe? The registry_id is \"B\". The cases have capacity 3 and 7 trials each. We need to produce memory with at most 3 entries. The output must have signatures list as well: \"signatures\" is an array of strings, likely the list of signatures in the memory entries? Possibly just the signature field? Or all signatures? The output schema has \"memory\" and \"signatures\" as required arrays. \"signatures\" likely is a list of signature strings (maybe the ones that appear in memory). But it could be separate.\n\nGiven the instruction: \"Return complete retained entries and their signatures.\" So memory has entries, signatures is array of signature strings from those entries. So signatures are just the signature field of each memory entry.\n\nNow design algorithm:\n\nInitialize empty list memory.\n\nFor each trial in trials (index i):\n  trial has signature, reflection, success.\n  Create entry: {\"last_trial\": i, \"reflection\": trial[\"reflection\"], \"signature\": trial[\"signature\"], \"success\": trial[\"success\"]}\n  Goal: maintain memory list that after each trial might be updated.\n\nDeduplication: If success is False (failure) and there is an existing entry in memory with same signature and success=False, then replace that entry with the new one (keeping last_trial = current i, reflection = new reflection, signature same, success=False). This ensures only most recent failure reflection for each signature is kept. For successes, we don't dedup? Probably no dedup for successes. But if a success has same signature as another success, do we keep both? Possibly yes. The description says \"by failure signature\", so only failures dedup.\n\nAfter processing all trials, we have memory list (may exceed capacity). Then truncate to capacity. Truncation rule: \"prioritize failures before successes, then recency and signature.\" So we want to keep as many failures as possible (up to capacity), and if capacity still available, keep successes. Among failures, we need to keep the most recent ones? \"then recency and signature\" could mean: within failures, sort by recency (descending: most recent first) and if tie, by signature (lexicographic). But we need to retain entries, not just sort. When truncating to capacity, we want to keep the top capacity entries after sorting by priority: first all failures (sorted by recency descending, then signature), then successes (sorted similarly). But also we might need to consider that we already deduped failures. So final memory will have at most capacity entries.\n\nWe also need to restore chronological order in the final memory. So after selecting which entries to keep, we sort them by last_trial ascending (original chronological order).\n\nEdge cases: capacity may be 0? Possibly.\n\nThe output must have \"memory\" array of objects (with required fields) and \"signatures\" array of strings (may be same order as memory? Or sorted? The schema doesn't specify order, but likely order of memory entries. Since we restore chronological order, signatures will be in chronological order.\n\nImplementation steps in solve(case):\n\n- Validate that case has \"capacity\" and \"trials\". Must be present. Raise ValueError if missing or invalid.\n- Initialize memory = [].\n- For idx, trial in enumerate(trials):\n    entry = {\"last_trial\": idx, \"reflection\": trial[\"reflection\"], \"signature\": trial[\"signature\"], \"success\": trial[\"success\"]}\n    if trial[\"success\"] == False:\n        # Check for existing failure with same signature\n        found = False\n        for i, mem in enumerate(memory):\n            if not mem[\"success\"] and mem[\"signature\"] == trial[\"signature\"]:\n                # Replace\n                memory[i] = entry\n                found = True\n                break\n        if not found:\n            memory.append(entry)\n    else:\n        # Success: simply add\n        memory.append(entry)\n- After all trials, apply capacity bound.\n- If len(memory) <= capacity: keep all.\n- Else:\n    # Separate failures and successes\n    failures = [m for m in memory if not m[\"success\"]]\n    successes = [m for m in memory if m[\"success\"]]\n    # Sort each group: first by recency (last_trial descending), then by signature lexicographic (ascending)\n    failures.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[\"last_trial\"], x[\"signature\"]))\n    successes.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[\"last_trial\"], x[\"signature\"]))\n    # Combine failures then successes\n    sorted_all = failures + successes\n    # Keep first capacity\n    kept = sorted_all[:capacity]\n    # Restore chronological order (by last_trial ascending)\n    kept.sort(key=lambda x: x[\"last_trial\"])\n    memory = kept\n- Extract signatures = [m[\"signature\"] for m in memory]\n- Return {\"memory\": memory, \"signatures\": signatures}\n\nBut we need to ensure that deduplication does not affect capacity count? The dedup already reduces size. Also, note that if a failure appears multiple times, we replace the entry, so we don't increase count. That's fine.\n\nPotential alternative: dedup after truncation? But the instruction says \"deduplicate memory by failure signature, retaining the newest reflection.\" That suggests before capacity, because we want to keep the newest reflection. So our approach is correct.\n\nNow, we must produce Python code that implements this, with proper error handling. The function must return a JSON-serializable object. Use only standard library.\n\nWe also need to follow output structural schema: memory items must have last_trial (int), reflection (str), signature (str), success (bool). Ensure that any extra keys (like from trial) are not added. The case may have \"_case_nonce\" but that's not in trial. We use only trial fields.\n\nWe'll also need to handle case where capacity is negative? Probably not but raise ValueError.\n\nNow produce the final JSON with key \"implementation\" and value the Python source string.\n\nThe source must be inside a JSON string, so we need to escape newlines, backslashes, quotes. We'll use triple quotes in Python but in JSON we need to escape. We'll write the Python code as a string and then use json.dumps to escape properly. But since we are generating text, we need to manually escape or rely on our own escaping. We'll write the code with proper indentation, then escape newlines as \\n, double quotes as \\\", backslashes as \\\\.\n\nWe'll produce final JSON response.\n\nLet's write the Python code:\n\n```python\ndef solve(case):\n    # Validate required fields\n    if \"capacity\" not in case or \"trials\" not in case:\n        raise ValueError(\"Missing required keys: capacity, trials\")\n    capacity = case[\"capacity\"]\n    trials = case[\"trials\"]\n    if not isinstance(capacity, int) or capacity < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Capacity must be non-negative integer\")\n    if not isinstance(trials, list):\n        raise ValueError(\"Trials must be a list\")\n\n    memory = []\n\n    for idx, trial in enumerate(trials):\n        # Validate trial fields\n        if not isinstance(trial, dict) or not {\"signature\", \"reflection\", \"success\"}.issubset(trial.keys()):\n            raise ValueError(\"Each trial must have signature, reflection, success\")\n        entry = {\n            \"last_trial\": idx,\n            \"reflection\": trial[\"reflection\"],\n            \"signature\": trial[\"signature\"],\n            \"success\": trial[\"success\"]\n        }\n        # Deduplicate failures by signature\n        if not trial[\"success\"]:\n            replaced = False\n            for i, mem in enumerate(memory):\n                if not mem[\"success\"] and mem[\"signature\"] == trial[\"signature\"]:\n                    memory[i] = entry\n                    replaced = True\n                    break\n            if not replaced:\n                memory.append(entry)\n        else:\n            memory.append(entry)\n\n    # Truncate if over capacity\n    if len(memory) > capacity:\n        failures = [m for m in memory if not m[\"success\"]]\n        successes = [m for m in memory if m[\"success\"]]\n        # Sort: recency descending (last_trial), then signature ascending\n        failures.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[\"last_trial\"], x[\"signature\"]))\n        successes.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[\"last_trial\"], x[\"signature\"]))\n        sorted_all = failures + successes\n        kept = sorted_all[:capacity]\n        # Restore chronological order\n        kept.sort(key=lambda x: x[\"last_trial\"])\n        memory = kept\n\n    signatures = [m[\"signature\"] for m in memory]\n    return {\"memory\": memory, \"signatures\": signatures}\n```\n\nWe need to ensure that we don't mutate case input. We are not modifying.\n\nNow, we must produce JSON with \"implementation\" key. We'll convert this Python code into a valid JSON string. We'll write the code as a double-quoted string with proper escaping.\n\nWe'll output exactly:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    if \\\"capacity\\\" not in case or \\\"trials\\\" not in case:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Missing required keys: capacity, trials\\\")\\n    capacity = case[\\\"capacity\\\"]\\n    trials = case[\\\"trials\\\"]\\n    if not isinstance(capacity, int) or capacity < 0:\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Capacity must be non-negative integer\\\")\\n    if not isinstance(trials, list):\\n        raise ValueError(\\\"Trials must be a list\\\")\\n\\n    memory = []\\n\\n    for idx, trial in enumerate(trials):\\n        if not isinstance(trial, dict) or not {\\\"signature\\\", \\\"reflection\\\", \\\"success\\\"}.issubset(trial.keys()):\\n            raise ValueError(\\\"Each trial must have signature, reflection, success\\\")\\n        entry = {\\n            \\\"last_trial\\\": idx,\\n            \\\"reflection\\\": trial[\\\"reflection\\\"],\\n            \\\"signature\\\": trial[\\\"signature\\\"],\\n            \\\"success\\\": trial[\\\"success\\\"]\\n        }\\n        if not trial[\\\"success\\\"]:\\n            replaced = False\\n            for i, mem in enumerate(memory):\\n                if not mem[\\\"success\\\"] and mem[\\\"signature\\\"] == trial[\\\"signature\\\"]:\\n                    memory[i] = entry\\n                    replaced = True\\n                    break\\n            if not replaced:\\n                memory.append(entry)\\n        else:\\n            memory.append(entry)\\n\\n    if len(memory) > capacity:\\n        failures = [m for m in memory if not m[\\\"success\\\"]]\\n        successes = [m for m in memory if m[\\\"success\\\"]]\\n        failures.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[\\\"last_trial\\\"], x[\\\"signature\\\"]))\\n        successes.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[\\\"last_trial\\\"], x[\\\"signature\\\"]))\\n        sorted_all = failures + successes\\n        kept = sorted_all[:capacity]\\n        kept.sort(key=lambda x: x[\\\"last_trial\\\"])\\n        memory = kept\\n\\n    signatures = [m[\\\"signature\\\"] for m in memory]\\n    return {\\\"memory\\\": memory, \\\"signatures\\\": signatures}\"}\n\nBut we need to check for any unescaped quotes within. For instance, the set {\"signature\", \"reflection\", \"success\"} contains double quotes. We need to escape them as \\\". Also the internal strings like \"last_trial\" etc. The code as written uses double quotes, so we need to escape each \" as \\\". Also backslashes: we have no backslashes except in the lambda? No backslashes. Newlines we already have \\n. So we can write the JSON string with escaped newlines and quotes.\n\nLet's produce the final JSON. Ensure no trailing spaces, and exactly one JSON object.\n\nWe need to be careful with indentation. We'll use a single line for the Python code can be compact, but we can keep newlines for readability. The JSON string will contain newlines as \\n.\n\nI'll write the implementation as a string with proper escaping.\n\nFinal output."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1726,"completion_tokens":3864,"total_tokens":5590,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1664},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_to